  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Sat Jun 20 2026\n\nBroad, elongated subtropical high remains fixed over Sonora/Gulf\nof California, leading to a stagnant surface high over Arizona\nthis weekend. Prevailing west/northwest flow has scoured out \nmoisture allowing precipitable water to fall to 0.6-0.8\". 00Z KEPZ\nsounding confirms this dry-out. No rain chances Sunday through at\nleast next Tuesday because of this.\n\nMain headline over the next several days will be heat. \nSubtropical high will slowly progress over the region this \nweekend, centering directly overhead by Monday-Tuesday. Lowland \ntemperatures will reach or surpass 100 degrees each afternoon and\nlooks to stay that way through the week ahead. The hottest days \nwill be Monday-Wednesday, with the Rio Grande Valley reaching \n104-108 degrees. While this is typical for late June, it's still \nimpactful heat. Heat Advisories will be needed for El Paso, and \nperhaps much of the I-10/I-25 corridors.\n\nShortwaves moving across the Central Rockies midweek will flatten\nthe subtropical high and weaken it somewhat after next Wednesday.\nThis should allow temperatures to \"cool\" a few degrees, though \nstill likely above 100 degrees for El Paso and the Rio Grande \nValley. Slight increases in moisture will also allow for the \nreturn of thunderstorm chances Wednesday-Friday next week. \nCurrently, this looks to be mostly limited to the high terrain \n(Black Range, Sacramento Mountains), with most of the lowlands \nremaining dry and hot into the final week of June.",
